1
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:03,394
[instrumental music]

2
00:00:03,481 --> 00:00:05,005
[seagulls squawking]

3
00:00:08,747 --> 00:00:10,706
[indistinct
police radio chatter]

4
00:00:13,187 --> 00:00:14,579
Lovely.

5
00:00:14,666 --> 00:00:17,713
Yeah. Used to be a fluorescent
light bulb factory.

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,932
Now it's a hazardous waste site.
There's mercury dust everywhere.

7
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:21,195
- Great.
- Yeah.

8
00:00:21,282 --> 00:00:22,718
It's probably
not too dangerous

9
00:00:22,805 --> 00:00:24,763
as long as you don't,
you know, breathe.

10
00:00:27,723 --> 00:00:29,420
Some techs from the
hazardous materials office

11
00:00:29,507 --> 00:00:31,161
came in to test the air.

12
00:00:31,248 --> 00:00:33,903
Saw a lock on one of the
lockers, busted it open.

13
00:00:33,990 --> 00:00:35,513
'That's when they found it.'

14
00:00:37,385 --> 00:00:38,864
How long does the coroner think
he's been in there?

15
00:00:38,951 --> 00:00:40,214
He said with that level
of decomp

16
00:00:40,301 --> 00:00:41,954
at least six months.
Could be longer.

17
00:00:42,042 --> 00:00:43,826
Handcuffs on the wrists,
ankles.

18
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:45,523
Did he have any clothes
or personal effects?

19
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:49,440
This. We found it
inside his ribcage.

20
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:51,355
[Lisbon]
'Probably something medical.'

21
00:00:51,442 --> 00:00:54,228
Have the coroner's assistant
look at it.

22
00:00:54,315 --> 00:00:57,274
Scratches on the doors.
Must be from his cuffs.

23
00:00:57,361 --> 00:00:59,711
He was alive
when he was put inside.

24
00:00:59,798 --> 00:01:02,105
How long would it take to die
in here? Like, five, six days?

25
00:01:02,192 --> 00:01:04,586
That's the right place for it.
No one in earshot.

26
00:01:04,673 --> 00:01:06,544
Make sure the techs search
every inch of this floor

27
00:01:06,631 --> 00:01:07,937
and the one above.

28
00:01:08,024 --> 00:01:09,243
[cell phone rings]

29
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:10,853
[sighs]

30
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:15,162
- What's up? Where are you?
- Yeah. Sorry, Lisbon.

31
00:01:15,249 --> 00:01:16,902
I had my phone turned off.

32
00:01:16,989 --> 00:01:19,209
We're in the middle
of a toxic waste dump. Hurry up.

33
00:01:19,296 --> 00:01:22,299
Ooh. That sounds inviting.
I'll see you there.

34
00:01:22,386 --> 00:01:24,519
'Chop chop.
Time's a-wastin'.'

35
00:01:28,175 --> 00:01:30,264
[instrumental music]

36
00:01:43,712 --> 00:01:45,279
[engine starts]

37
00:01:52,547 --> 00:01:54,244
[birds chirping]

38
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,762
[music continues]

39
00:02:42,945 --> 00:02:44,512
[Hailey]
'Hello, Patrick.'

40
00:02:47,863 --> 00:02:51,171
Hello.

41
00:02:51,258 --> 00:02:53,434
What's your name?

42
00:02:53,521 --> 00:02:54,826
Hailey.

43
00:02:54,913 --> 00:02:56,350
Well, it's nice to meet you,
Hailey.

44
00:02:59,091 --> 00:03:01,442
How'd you know
my name was Patrick?

45
00:03:01,529 --> 00:03:02,617
Your friend told me.

46
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:07,404
Which friend's that?

47
00:03:07,491 --> 00:03:10,277
The one who brought me here.
He gave me this.

48
00:03:10,364 --> 00:03:12,192
[intense music]

49
00:03:16,935 --> 00:03:18,894
He didn't do anything else,
did he?

50
00:03:18,981 --> 00:03:22,245
He told me to ask you
a question.

51
00:03:22,332 --> 00:03:23,551
What's that?

52
00:03:23,638 --> 00:03:25,335
He said to ask you..

53
00:03:26,380 --> 00:03:28,295
...do you give up yet?

54
00:03:28,382 --> 00:03:30,035
[music continues]

55
00:03:36,781 --> 00:03:39,219
[instrumental music]

56
00:03:47,836 --> 00:03:49,577
Apparently, Red John
or someone pretending to be him

57
00:03:49,664 --> 00:03:51,796
lured the girl away from
a nearby park about an hour ago.

58
00:03:51,883 --> 00:03:53,755
Her class was on a field trip.

59
00:03:53,842 --> 00:03:56,236
It doesn't look like she was
harmed. Her mother's on her way.

60
00:03:56,323 --> 00:03:57,715
Where's Jane?

61
00:03:57,802 --> 00:03:59,804
Over there.
He hasn't said much.

62
00:04:01,589 --> 00:04:02,633
Gonna run this note
they left for Jane

63
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,069
check it for prints andDNA.

64
00:04:04,156 --> 00:04:05,723
You won't get any.

65
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:06,942
I know. It's worth a shot.

66
00:04:07,029 --> 00:04:08,683
"Happy anniversary."

67
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:10,815
Today's the day that his wife
and daughter..

68
00:04:10,902 --> 00:04:13,122
- I know. Nine years ago.
- Yeah.

69
00:04:13,209 --> 00:04:15,167
- Would you excuse me, sir?
- Yeah.

70
00:04:17,996 --> 00:04:19,259
Hey there.

71
00:04:19,346 --> 00:04:20,651
Hey there, yourself.

72
00:04:22,479 --> 00:04:25,439
Hailey, this is, uh,
my friend Teresa.

73
00:04:25,526 --> 00:04:27,658
- Hi, Hailey.
- I like your hair.

74
00:04:27,745 --> 00:04:29,617
Thank you.

75
00:04:29,704 --> 00:04:33,316
Patrick is teaching me
how to make a quarter disappear.

76
00:04:33,403 --> 00:04:34,796
- The kid's a natural.
- No doubt.

77
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:39,540
Hailey, hello.
My name's Agent Wainwright.

78
00:04:39,627 --> 00:04:41,193
Your mom's gonna
be here soon.

79
00:04:41,281 --> 00:04:42,630
'When she gets here,
we're gonna talk to you'

80
00:04:42,717 --> 00:04:44,893
'about the man you met.
Is that alright?'

81
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:47,330
What man?

82
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:50,028
The one who sent you
to Mr. Jane

83
00:04:50,115 --> 00:04:51,900
who drew the face
on your hand.

84
00:04:54,206 --> 00:04:55,904
I don't know
what you're talking about.

85
00:04:58,646 --> 00:05:00,517
Hailey, why don't you go
and show that policewoman

86
00:05:00,604 --> 00:05:01,953
your new trick?

87
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:03,390
- Okay.
- Have fun.

88
00:05:07,219 --> 00:05:10,701
Um, I...I don't understand.
She doesn't remember?

89
00:05:10,788 --> 00:05:12,877
You hypnotized her.

90
00:05:12,964 --> 00:05:16,707
No, I just planted a suggestion
for her to forget Red John.

91
00:05:16,794 --> 00:05:19,623
Why would you do that?
She could identify him.

92
00:05:19,710 --> 00:05:21,799
He's white.
He wore a baseball cap.

93
00:05:21,886 --> 00:05:23,627
He had an odd voice.

94
00:05:23,714 --> 00:05:25,150
Alright, look,
she might remember more

95
00:05:25,237 --> 00:05:26,238
if she were
questioned properly.

96
00:05:26,326 --> 00:05:28,719
Jane. Jane, she's a-a witness.

97
00:05:28,806 --> 00:05:32,375
People who can identify Red John
end up dead.

98
00:05:32,462 --> 00:05:33,681
I get that you feel
protective here.

99
00:05:33,768 --> 00:05:34,899
Do you?

100
00:05:34,986 --> 00:05:36,553
- This is not your call.
- Yes, it is.

101
00:05:36,640 --> 00:05:39,513
If you try to question her
or even talk to her

102
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:40,905
I will hurt you badly.

103
00:05:44,213 --> 00:05:45,214
Did he really
just threaten me?

104
00:05:47,259 --> 00:05:51,002
Jane, I know it's hard coming
here, especially like this

105
00:05:51,089 --> 00:05:53,353
and I'm sorry.

106
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,354
I'm not gonna ask you
to apologize

107
00:05:54,441 --> 00:05:56,138
to Wainwright
but he's right.

108
00:05:56,225 --> 00:05:59,794
The little girl's a witness.
We should interview her.

109
00:05:59,881 --> 00:06:01,622
You know what Red John's
trying to do, don't you?

110
00:06:03,232 --> 00:06:04,581
He asked me
if I'm ready to quit.

111
00:06:04,668 --> 00:06:07,236
He's messing with your head.
Don't let him.

112
00:06:08,933 --> 00:06:11,066
- You okay?
- Yeah, I'm-I'm fine.

113
00:06:11,153 --> 00:06:12,459
[cell phone ringing]

114
00:06:12,546 --> 00:06:15,375
Appreciate your concern.
Your phone is ringing.

115
00:06:15,462 --> 00:06:17,812
[ringing continues]

116
00:06:17,899 --> 00:06:18,856
Hey, Rigsby.

117
00:06:20,554 --> 00:06:22,512
So that thing that we found was
a surgical heart valve

118
00:06:22,599 --> 00:06:24,384
implanted in the victim.

119
00:06:24,471 --> 00:06:27,212
Traced the serial number on it
to a guy named Antonio Castro.

120
00:06:27,299 --> 00:06:29,519
According to SFPD,
he disappeared 11 months ago.

121
00:06:29,606 --> 00:06:31,260
[Lisbon]
Disappeared how?

122
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:33,654
He sent some e-mails from his
home on the night of June 17th.

123
00:06:33,741 --> 00:06:35,351
'The next morning,
he was gone.'

124
00:06:35,438 --> 00:06:36,831
'Car parked in the driveway.
Front door was locked.'

125
00:06:36,918 --> 00:06:38,659
'No sign of forced entry
or struggle.'

126
00:06:38,746 --> 00:06:40,704
His fiance filed a missing
persons report that afternoon.

127
00:06:40,791 --> 00:06:42,358
SFPD find anything?

128
00:06:42,445 --> 00:06:44,839
No. No contact with the family.
They're out of state.

129
00:06:44,926 --> 00:06:47,581
No travel plans,
no enemies, no money troubles.

130
00:06:47,668 --> 00:06:48,973
[Lisbon]
What'd he do for a living?

131
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:51,236
He's former military.
Two tours in Iraq.

132
00:06:51,323 --> 00:06:52,977
'The past five years,
he's been working as a broker'

133
00:06:53,064 --> 00:06:55,066
'in a financial company,
RF Victor.'

134
00:06:55,153 --> 00:06:57,199
[Lisbon]
'Van Pelt, bring in
all the SFPD files'

135
00:06:57,286 --> 00:06:58,766
and any physical evidence.

136
00:06:58,853 --> 00:07:00,507
Cho, Rigsby,
I want you to put together

137
00:07:00,594 --> 00:07:02,596
a time line and a list
of all the contacts

138
00:07:02,683 --> 00:07:04,032
the victim had
before he disappeared.

139
00:07:04,119 --> 00:07:05,816
We need to interview everybody.

140
00:07:05,903 --> 00:07:07,775
Jane and I will go talk
to the people he worked with.

141
00:07:07,862 --> 00:07:11,387
Jane.
Anything to offer?

142
00:07:11,474 --> 00:07:13,998
Uh, well, there's not a lot
to go off, is there?

143
00:07:14,912 --> 00:07:16,087
Your point being..

144
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:20,265
No point.
Shall we go?

145
00:07:20,352 --> 00:07:21,745
Let's go.

146
00:07:24,748 --> 00:07:26,358
Jane seem off to you?

147
00:07:27,708 --> 00:07:28,709
Always.

148
00:07:32,364 --> 00:07:34,628
[telephone ringing]

149
00:07:34,715 --> 00:07:36,499
[indistinct chattering]

150
00:07:39,589 --> 00:07:43,506
Oh. Dennis Victor.
Uh, you're from, uh, CBI?

151
00:07:43,593 --> 00:07:46,117
Hi. I'm AgentLisbon,
and this is Patrick Jane.

152
00:07:46,204 --> 00:07:49,120
You're absolutely positive
that you found Antonio's body?

153
00:07:49,207 --> 00:07:50,861
Yes, sir. It was confirmed.

154
00:07:50,948 --> 00:07:52,689
[sighs]
Well, we all knew
this day could come.

155
00:07:52,776 --> 00:07:54,517
Still, a shock.

156
00:07:54,604 --> 00:07:56,737
He was engaged to my niece.

157
00:07:56,824 --> 00:07:58,347
I feel it very personally.
This way.

158
00:08:00,305 --> 00:08:03,308
[Lisbon]
'What exactly do you
do here, Mr. Victor?'

159
00:08:03,395 --> 00:08:05,093
[Victor]
'We're a financial derivatives
broker.'

160
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:08,705
'We offer
exchange-traded derivatives'

161
00:08:08,792 --> 00:08:13,275
options, futures, and a few
over-the-counter products.

162
00:08:13,362 --> 00:08:15,320
So basically,
you're just thieves

163
00:08:15,407 --> 00:08:18,236
in, uh, ties and suspenders.

164
00:08:18,323 --> 00:08:20,935
[laughs]
That would be
the popular view.

165
00:08:21,022 --> 00:08:22,937
Not entirely accurate.

166
00:08:23,024 --> 00:08:24,329
Up this way.

167
00:08:28,377 --> 00:08:30,292
Agent Lisbon

168
00:08:30,379 --> 00:08:32,599
Mr. Jane,
Ian Breitler.

169
00:08:32,686 --> 00:08:34,078
He recruited
Antonio to the firm

170
00:08:34,165 --> 00:08:35,776
'and he mentored him
during his first year.'

171
00:08:35,863 --> 00:08:37,473
It's great to meet you.
Thanks for coming.

172
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:40,128
This is Ben Marx. He runs
the division Antonio was in.

173
00:08:40,215 --> 00:08:41,651
He was his supervisor.

174
00:08:41,738 --> 00:08:43,958
- Welcome.
- Sit. Please, sit.

175
00:08:46,221 --> 00:08:47,309
[clears throat]
Mr. Jane.

176
00:08:47,396 --> 00:08:49,180
Oh, I'm fine. Thank you.

177
00:08:49,267 --> 00:08:51,313
What did Antonio Castro
do for you here?

178
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:54,359
He-he was a broker.
Handled customer accounts.

179
00:08:54,446 --> 00:08:55,970
How'd he get along
with people?

180
00:08:56,057 --> 00:08:59,713
Oh, he was universally loved,
a leader in the company.

181
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:00,757
[Ian]
'Absolutely.'

182
00:09:01,758 --> 00:09:03,325
You don't agree?

183
00:09:03,412 --> 00:09:05,675
Well, who's universally loved?
He was popular.

184
00:09:07,329 --> 00:09:09,244
Do you know anything
about his family?

185
00:09:09,331 --> 00:09:13,204
His mother's in a nursing home
in Flagstaff. Alzheimer's.

186
00:09:13,291 --> 00:09:16,904
And his sister converted to
Islam and moved to Indonesia.

187
00:09:16,991 --> 00:09:18,383
They weren't in touch.

188
00:09:18,470 --> 00:09:20,690
And did he have
any problems at work?

189
00:09:20,777 --> 00:09:22,866
Not that I was aware of.

190
00:09:22,953 --> 00:09:27,088
No. All of his performance
reviews were exemplary.

191
00:09:27,175 --> 00:09:28,132
Isn't that right, Ben?

192
00:09:28,219 --> 00:09:29,743
If there were any issues

193
00:09:29,830 --> 00:09:31,135
I wasn't aware of them
at the time.

194
00:09:31,222 --> 00:09:33,964
- Mm. Interesting.
- Excuse me?

195
00:09:34,051 --> 00:09:35,879
Well, he's telling the truth,
but you two aren't.

196
00:09:35,966 --> 00:09:37,968
Something going on with Castro,
what was the big secret?

197
00:09:39,579 --> 00:09:41,058
There wasn't one,
I assure you.

198
00:09:41,145 --> 00:09:43,452
- Yes, there was.
- I'm afraid you're mistaken.

199
00:09:43,539 --> 00:09:44,975
No, I'm-I'm sure,
just as I'm sure

200
00:09:45,062 --> 00:09:46,716
you're not very fond
of Mr. Breitler here.

201
00:09:46,803 --> 00:09:48,022
I'm Marx. He's Breitler.

202
00:09:48,109 --> 00:09:49,371
Yes, well, whatever
your name is

203
00:09:49,458 --> 00:09:51,155
your boss doesn't
like you very much

204
00:09:51,242 --> 00:09:53,244
and I wouldn't be surprised
if he canned you very soon.

205
00:09:53,331 --> 00:09:55,595
[Victor]
'That's a very interesting
observation because, uh'

206
00:09:55,682 --> 00:09:59,555
Ben here was made a principal
in the firm last month

207
00:09:59,642 --> 00:10:01,557
at my express request.

208
00:10:07,998 --> 00:10:09,217
Okay.

209
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:14,048
- What's going on, Jane?
- Nothing.

210
00:10:14,135 --> 00:10:16,267
Oh, I may be a little out of
sorts, but it's nothing serious.

211
00:10:16,354 --> 00:10:17,617
Wanna get something to eat?

212
00:10:17,704 --> 00:10:19,967
No, I think I might take
the rest of the day.

213
00:10:20,054 --> 00:10:21,446
- You sure?
- Yeah, I'm gonna get a cab.

214
00:10:21,533 --> 00:10:22,796
I'll see you tomorrow,
Lisbon.

215
00:10:31,674 --> 00:10:33,502
Hello, Mr. Jane.
Good to see you again.

216
00:10:33,589 --> 00:10:35,243
- You, too.
- Your table's ready.

217
00:10:36,287 --> 00:10:37,593
Thank you.

218
00:10:41,597 --> 00:10:43,512
And your order
will be right up.

219
00:10:43,599 --> 00:10:45,122
Thank you.

220
00:10:47,908 --> 00:10:49,561
[instrumental music]

221
00:11:45,617 --> 00:11:47,750
[music continues]

222
00:12:08,728 --> 00:12:10,207
[door rattles]

223
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:28,225
[sighs]

224
00:12:37,147 --> 00:12:38,758
[dramatic music]

225
00:13:45,128 --> 00:13:46,347
[Wainwright]
'Security tells me
the fire Jane started'

226
00:13:46,434 --> 00:13:47,652
'could've burned down
the building.'

227
00:13:47,739 --> 00:13:49,829
I know, sir.

228
00:13:49,916 --> 00:13:51,787
I could suspend Jane
for this

229
00:13:51,874 --> 00:13:55,225
but I think what happened
was a cry for help.

230
00:13:55,312 --> 00:13:58,141
I just need to find out
what the issue is.

231
00:13:58,228 --> 00:13:59,751
Me, too, sir.

232
00:13:59,839 --> 00:14:01,797
If you don't mind, I think
I should do the talking.

233
00:14:06,541 --> 00:14:08,499
Jane.

234
00:14:08,586 --> 00:14:09,892
Mm.

235
00:14:12,721 --> 00:14:14,201
[inhales deeply]

236
00:14:15,463 --> 00:14:16,638
What's going on?

237
00:14:17,944 --> 00:14:21,643
Well..

238
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:24,428
...I have a little hangover

239
00:14:24,515 --> 00:14:27,388
uh, if you must know.

240
00:14:27,475 --> 00:14:29,085
Why did you burn
your Red John files?

241
00:14:29,869 --> 00:14:31,348
[exhales deeply]

242
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,229
Because he's right.

243
00:14:44,927 --> 00:14:46,015
It's time to give up.

244
00:14:47,582 --> 00:14:48,757
Seriously?

245
00:14:51,281 --> 00:14:52,935
What do you mean?

246
00:14:53,022 --> 00:14:54,197
Well, nothing's working.

247
00:14:56,852 --> 00:14:58,027
Nothing.

248
00:15:00,290 --> 00:15:02,292
It's just a game,
and he keeps winning.

249
00:15:08,211 --> 00:15:10,822
The only way for me to stop him
is if I stop playing.

250
00:15:12,563 --> 00:15:13,782
Meaning what?

251
00:15:17,873 --> 00:15:19,353
Meaning I put it behind me.

252
00:15:22,791 --> 00:15:23,966
I move on.

253
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:28,840
That's a big change, Jane.

254
00:15:29,624 --> 00:15:31,713
[scoffs]

255
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:32,932
Well, change is good.

256
00:15:34,324 --> 00:15:35,369
Isn't it?

257
00:15:36,805 --> 00:15:40,417
So...are you
leaving the CBI?

258
00:15:40,504 --> 00:15:42,811
No, of course not.
What would I do for amusement?

259
00:15:42,898 --> 00:15:44,421
Speaking of which,
isn't there some person

260
00:15:44,508 --> 00:15:47,207
or something we should be
investigating or such?

261
00:15:47,294 --> 00:15:48,860
We're supposed to talk
to the victim's fiance.

262
00:15:48,948 --> 00:15:50,297
Okay. Let's go.

263
00:15:50,384 --> 00:15:52,212
Jane, I strongly recommend

264
00:15:52,299 --> 00:15:54,344
that you talk to one of our
counselors about what happened.

265
00:15:54,431 --> 00:15:56,390
I think it could help you.

266
00:15:56,477 --> 00:15:59,001
Luther, there's no time.
I got perps to break.

267
00:16:02,962 --> 00:16:05,660
Hey. How's the time line going?
Anything jump out?

268
00:16:05,747 --> 00:16:08,924
Yeah. Castro did completely
normal stuff, then disappeared.

269
00:16:09,011 --> 00:16:10,665
He was at a charity event,
right before he vanished, yeah?

270
00:16:10,752 --> 00:16:12,101
What happened there?

271
00:16:12,188 --> 00:16:14,408
It was a fund-raiser
for the homeless.

272
00:16:14,495 --> 00:16:18,629
[sighs]
Witnesses say Castro was
calm, relaxed, left alone.

273
00:16:18,716 --> 00:16:20,109
Are we gonna catch any
good breaks in this case?

274
00:16:20,196 --> 00:16:22,155
I doubt it.

275
00:16:22,242 --> 00:16:25,288
- What's the package?
- It's a baby monitor.

276
00:16:25,375 --> 00:16:27,595
It's got audio and video.

277
00:16:27,682 --> 00:16:29,597
Plus, it's 2-way,
so I can talk to Ben in his crib

278
00:16:29,684 --> 00:16:31,294
without leaving my bed.

279
00:16:31,381 --> 00:16:32,904
That should terrify him.

280
00:16:32,992 --> 00:16:36,038
Hey. So I've been going through
the stuff SFPD collected

281
00:16:36,125 --> 00:16:40,434
at Castro's office
and I found this, "19:24."

282
00:16:40,521 --> 00:16:43,089
It was stuck
to a folder on his desk.

283
00:16:43,176 --> 00:16:45,395
He was ex-military.
Could it be military time?

284
00:16:45,482 --> 00:16:47,093
No, there wouldn't be a colon
between the nine and two.

285
00:16:47,180 --> 00:16:48,572
It's something else.

286
00:16:48,659 --> 00:16:50,705
Well, if you have any ideas,
let me know.

287
00:16:50,792 --> 00:16:52,576
I'm not finding
anything else useful.

288
00:16:52,663 --> 00:16:55,144
You know that Jane told Lisbon
he was giving up on Red John?

289
00:16:55,231 --> 00:16:56,885
- Yeah.
- It's hard to believe.

290
00:16:59,192 --> 00:17:02,238
You think he's losing it?
Like cracking up?

291
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:04,632
No.

292
00:17:04,719 --> 00:17:06,025
- Nah.
- Nah.

293
00:17:09,071 --> 00:17:11,030
Thank you for talking to us,
Miss Victor.

294
00:17:11,117 --> 00:17:14,424
Of course. I would've met you
at the office but I jus..

295
00:17:14,511 --> 00:17:17,123
I couldn't go in
after I heard the news.

296
00:17:17,210 --> 00:17:19,081
How long were
you and Mr. Castro engaged?

297
00:17:19,168 --> 00:17:20,604
Almost a year.

298
00:17:20,691 --> 00:17:23,520
We'd been together
about two years before that.

299
00:17:23,607 --> 00:17:25,131
And how did you meet?

300
00:17:25,218 --> 00:17:27,829
Through work.
I'm with a PR agency

301
00:17:27,916 --> 00:17:32,486
that handles financial firms,
including my uncle's.

302
00:17:32,573 --> 00:17:37,230
Most of the guys that work in
finance are overgrown frat boys

303
00:17:37,317 --> 00:17:39,101
but..

304
00:17:39,188 --> 00:17:40,972
...Antonio was a man.

305
00:17:41,060 --> 00:17:44,715
Maybe it was his time in the
service. I don't know but..

306
00:17:44,802 --> 00:17:46,804
[exhales sharply]

307
00:17:48,110 --> 00:17:50,547
...he was a great soul.

308
00:17:50,634 --> 00:17:53,289
He was wise
and kind.

309
00:17:55,552 --> 00:17:57,424
I'm sorry.

310
00:17:57,511 --> 00:17:59,078
I thought
I was ready for this.

311
00:18:01,776 --> 00:18:02,777
Did he have any enemies?

312
00:18:04,344 --> 00:18:07,216
Antonio had a very strong sense
of morality.

313
00:18:07,303 --> 00:18:09,262
He believed in
right and wrong

314
00:18:09,349 --> 00:18:11,133
and would tell you
which was which.

315
00:18:11,220 --> 00:18:14,615
I loved him for that,
but not everyone did.

316
00:18:15,964 --> 00:18:18,314
Anyone in particular?

317
00:18:18,401 --> 00:18:20,969
I can't think
of anyone offhand.

318
00:18:21,056 --> 00:18:24,320
Uh, Lisbon, my head's a little
foggy and, uh, drifty

319
00:18:24,407 --> 00:18:25,843
so I'm gonna push it
along a bit.

320
00:18:25,930 --> 00:18:27,149
What are you guilty about?

321
00:18:31,110 --> 00:18:32,676
I don't understand.

322
00:18:32,763 --> 00:18:34,939
The tears and all the talk
of how wonderful he was.

323
00:18:35,026 --> 00:18:36,811
He's been gone a year

324
00:18:36,898 --> 00:18:40,031
and you're behaving like
he disappeared this morning.

325
00:18:40,119 --> 00:18:42,556
- That's guilt.
- No, it's not.

326
00:18:42,643 --> 00:18:45,385
It's not quite homicidal,
but there's something.

327
00:18:45,472 --> 00:18:46,951
Were you cheating on him?

328
00:18:47,038 --> 00:18:49,171
- No.
- Not while he was alive.

329
00:18:49,258 --> 00:18:52,000
I bet now. Yes, that's it.

330
00:18:52,087 --> 00:18:55,351
You've been sleeping around
on the sainted dead fiance.

331
00:18:55,438 --> 00:18:57,484
- No, I haven't.
- Tell us the truth.

332
00:18:58,876 --> 00:19:00,095
[sighs]

333
00:19:00,182 --> 00:19:01,879
Well, it's complicated.

334
00:19:01,966 --> 00:19:03,054
It's simple.

335
00:19:03,142 --> 00:19:05,579
You've moved on. It happens.

336
00:19:05,666 --> 00:19:08,277
You just don't want to admit it
because you think it looks bad.

337
00:19:08,364 --> 00:19:09,670
Are you trying
to make me cry again?

338
00:19:09,757 --> 00:19:11,019
No, I-I'm not.
I mean, I'm not

339
00:19:11,106 --> 00:19:13,021
but if you want to, then..

340
00:19:13,108 --> 00:19:14,196
...please, cry.

341
00:19:14,283 --> 00:19:15,284
- Get it out.
- Jane.

342
00:19:17,547 --> 00:19:20,202
Who is it, Marcy?

343
00:19:20,289 --> 00:19:24,032
Hey, Cho, so Marcy Victor was
sleeping with Ian Breitler

344
00:19:24,119 --> 00:19:25,686
which Breitler conveniently
forgot to tell us

345
00:19:25,773 --> 00:19:27,296
when we talked to him.

346
00:19:27,383 --> 00:19:29,168
Pick him up. See what
he has to say for himself.

347
00:19:34,477 --> 00:19:36,044
Why did you hide your
relationship with Marcy Victor

348
00:19:36,131 --> 00:19:38,133
Mr. Breitler?

349
00:19:38,220 --> 00:19:41,745
Antonio was a sensitive topic
around the office, okay?

350
00:19:41,832 --> 00:19:43,617
Until you guys found him

351
00:19:43,704 --> 00:19:45,358
a lot of people were hoping
he'd come walking back

352
00:19:45,445 --> 00:19:46,663
through the door.

353
00:19:46,750 --> 00:19:48,578
Since you're sleeping
with his fiance

354
00:19:48,665 --> 00:19:50,711
I'm guessing
you're not one of 'em.

355
00:19:50,798 --> 00:19:51,842
I just didn't think
that the time to announce

356
00:19:51,929 --> 00:19:53,670
that Marcy and I
are a couple

357
00:19:53,757 --> 00:19:56,151
was when we had just found out
that Antonio was dead.

358
00:19:56,238 --> 00:19:57,500
Hey, Cho.

359
00:19:57,587 --> 00:20:00,590
Ian, what is that on your..

360
00:20:00,677 --> 00:20:01,635
...jacket?

361
00:20:01,722 --> 00:20:03,767
- There's...eh.
- What?

362
00:20:03,854 --> 00:20:07,075
It's a hair.
That could be a clue.

363
00:20:07,162 --> 00:20:08,468
It's my hair.

364
00:20:10,470 --> 00:20:12,428
Hmm. Fair enough.

365
00:20:12,515 --> 00:20:13,734
Ian doesn't think there's
anything significant

366
00:20:13,821 --> 00:20:15,866
about his relationship
with Marcy Victor.

367
00:20:15,953 --> 00:20:17,520
Yes, I'm inclined to agree.

368
00:20:17,607 --> 00:20:19,957
Sleazy, maybe.

369
00:20:20,044 --> 00:20:21,611
But motive for murder?
I don't think so.

370
00:20:21,698 --> 00:20:23,352
I'm more interested
in Ian's other secret.

371
00:20:24,832 --> 00:20:26,225
What's that?

372
00:20:26,312 --> 00:20:30,229
The one that you
and Dennis Victor were keeping.

373
00:20:30,316 --> 00:20:33,101
The one that has to do
with Antonio Castro's work.

374
00:20:34,755 --> 00:20:36,496
Don't know what
you're talking about.

375
00:20:36,583 --> 00:20:39,890
Oh.
You're tensing up, Ian.

376
00:20:39,977 --> 00:20:42,197
And you have asthma.
That's not good.

377
00:20:42,284 --> 00:20:44,504
That kind of tension goes
straight to the lungs

378
00:20:44,591 --> 00:20:48,290
squeezes them, makes them
get smaller and smaller.

379
00:20:49,683 --> 00:20:51,250
Is there a question here?

380
00:20:51,337 --> 00:20:52,860
The windpipe shrinks up

381
00:20:52,947 --> 00:20:54,992
and every breath you take
is harder than the last.

382
00:20:55,079 --> 00:20:56,342
It's hard to breathe.

383
00:20:56,429 --> 00:20:57,734
The last thing you
want to do is get upset.

384
00:20:57,821 --> 00:20:59,388
- This is ridiculous.
- Don't panic.

385
00:20:59,475 --> 00:21:03,087
What are you looking for?
What is it? Is it this?

386
00:21:03,174 --> 00:21:04,654
Gimme that.

387
00:21:04,741 --> 00:21:07,309
Tell me what I need to know.

388
00:21:07,396 --> 00:21:09,224
[sighs]
You can't do that.

389
00:21:09,311 --> 00:21:11,270
- He can't-he can't do that.
- He's doing it.

390
00:21:11,357 --> 00:21:12,749
[sighs]

391
00:21:12,836 --> 00:21:14,577
Yeah, you know,
you're probably right.

392
00:21:14,664 --> 00:21:17,798
- Changed my mind.
- For God sake.

393
00:21:17,885 --> 00:21:19,800
[sighs]
I'm calling my lawyer.

394
00:21:19,887 --> 00:21:22,193
Sure. Go ahead. It'll only take
him an hour to get here.

395
00:21:24,326 --> 00:21:25,588
[exhales deeply]

396
00:21:28,417 --> 00:21:29,505
[groans]

397
00:21:29,592 --> 00:21:30,637
Castro was a thief.

398
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:34,554
He was stealing
from the firm.

399
00:21:34,641 --> 00:21:37,252
We didn't find out
until after he disappeared

400
00:21:37,339 --> 00:21:39,123
when Marx took over
his accounts.

401
00:21:39,210 --> 00:21:40,473
Mm.

402
00:21:41,430 --> 00:21:43,127
Hey!
Come on!

403
00:21:44,694 --> 00:21:46,174
[exhales deeply]

404
00:21:48,916 --> 00:21:51,135
- How much did Castro steal?
- Breitler says he doesn't know.

405
00:21:51,222 --> 00:21:52,354
Marx and Dennis Victor
didn't share

406
00:21:52,441 --> 00:21:53,921
the numbers
with anyone else.

407
00:21:54,008 --> 00:21:55,662
Why didn't they tell us that
to start with?

408
00:21:55,749 --> 00:21:57,751
Victor's order. No one
was allowed to say anything.

409
00:21:57,838 --> 00:21:59,274
Alright, let's bring him in
and sort it out.

410
00:21:59,361 --> 00:22:00,667
- Sure.
- Hey.

411
00:22:03,365 --> 00:22:05,541
How does Jane seem to you?

412
00:22:05,628 --> 00:22:07,543
Different.
I think he's in trouble.

413
00:22:08,892 --> 00:22:11,199
Me, too. Where is he?

414
00:22:11,286 --> 00:22:13,462
He said he still had a headache.
Went out to go get some Aspirin.

415
00:22:13,549 --> 00:22:14,724
Alright.

416
00:22:16,900 --> 00:22:17,858
[birds chirping]

417
00:22:17,945 --> 00:22:19,250
Mr. Wineman.

418
00:22:20,687 --> 00:22:22,471
Beautiful cemetery.

419
00:22:22,558 --> 00:22:25,518
You should be proud.
So neat.

420
00:22:25,605 --> 00:22:29,348
Mm. Thank you. Uh, what-what
can I do for you, Mr. Jane?

421
00:22:29,435 --> 00:22:31,828
Well, like I said on the phone,
um, I'm with the CBI.

422
00:22:31,915 --> 00:22:34,178
We're pursuing
a particularly vicious killer.

423
00:22:35,179 --> 00:22:36,485
Tough case.

424
00:22:36,572 --> 00:22:38,487
But with your help,
we can crack it.

425
00:22:38,574 --> 00:22:41,055
You're not using this grave
right away, are you?

426
00:22:41,142 --> 00:22:44,058
No. The services aren't until
the day after tomorrow.

427
00:22:44,145 --> 00:22:45,233
Good.
We'll be done by then.

428
00:22:46,974 --> 00:22:48,410
Wh-what do you mean done?

429
00:22:50,151 --> 00:22:52,545
We need to run
a small police operation here.

430
00:22:52,632 --> 00:22:54,851
Nothing I can tell you about,
but you can help us.

431
00:22:54,938 --> 00:22:57,245
Help us remove a very dangerous
killer from the street.

432
00:22:57,332 --> 00:22:59,682
You want to help us,
don't you?

433
00:22:59,769 --> 00:23:01,554
You'll help us?

434
00:23:01,641 --> 00:23:05,384
- Help? Uh, y-yes. Of course.
- Good.

435
00:23:05,471 --> 00:23:09,083
We'll need a coffin as well.
Nothing too fancy.

436
00:23:09,170 --> 00:23:11,085
- A coffin?
- Yes. Down there.

437
00:23:17,178 --> 00:23:18,658
After Antonio vanished,
Ben here took over his accounts

438
00:23:18,745 --> 00:23:21,835
and four weeks in,
he found something.

439
00:23:21,922 --> 00:23:24,490
Antonio was stealing money
from customers

440
00:23:24,577 --> 00:23:27,449
and hiding it with paper losses
on fictional investments.

441
00:23:27,536 --> 00:23:29,973
He'd been doing it
for about six months.

442
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:31,235
- 'Mm.'
- How much money did he steal?

443
00:23:31,322 --> 00:23:32,715
Oh, we'll never be sure.

444
00:23:32,802 --> 00:23:35,022
Somewhere between
seven and ten million.

445
00:23:35,109 --> 00:23:36,980
- Where's the money?
- We have no idea.

446
00:23:37,067 --> 00:23:40,201
Antonio's transactions were
brilliantly complex.

447
00:23:40,288 --> 00:23:43,422
The money's
essentially untraceable.

448
00:23:43,509 --> 00:23:45,249
[Cho]
'This wasn't
in the SFPD files.'

449
00:23:45,336 --> 00:23:47,077
- 'Did you tell them?'
- 'No, they didn't ask.'

450
00:23:47,164 --> 00:23:49,515
And we didn't discover
the theft

451
00:23:49,602 --> 00:23:51,691
until after
they had interviewed us.

452
00:23:51,778 --> 00:23:53,562
You could've picked up
the phone.

453
00:23:53,649 --> 00:23:56,826
Agent, because we discovered
the theft internally

454
00:23:56,913 --> 00:23:59,699
we were able to make good
on our customers' losses.

455
00:23:59,786 --> 00:24:01,004
They don't even know
they were robbed.

456
00:24:01,091 --> 00:24:04,181
That cost my firm
a great deal of money

457
00:24:04,268 --> 00:24:07,620
but it saved us
major embarrassment.

458
00:24:07,707 --> 00:24:08,925
And I couldn't see any value
in publicizing

459
00:24:09,012 --> 00:24:12,320
what had been
so fortunately hidden.

460
00:24:12,407 --> 00:24:14,540
It was evidence
in a homicide.

461
00:24:14,627 --> 00:24:16,063
Well, we had to balance justice
with self-preservation.

462
00:24:16,150 --> 00:24:17,543
[Marx]
'Absolutely.'

463
00:24:17,630 --> 00:24:21,503
I think we found
a very good middle course.

464
00:24:21,590 --> 00:24:24,027
This is obstruction of justice,
and I'm gonna have you charged.

465
00:24:24,114 --> 00:24:26,465
I'm sorry. You-you asked
if we knew about anything

466
00:24:26,552 --> 00:24:28,684
Antonio had done at the time.

467
00:24:28,771 --> 00:24:31,600
Right? We said we didn't know
anything at the time.

468
00:24:31,687 --> 00:24:33,167
- Mm.
- All true.

469
00:24:33,254 --> 00:24:35,430
- Tell it to your lawyers.
- No need.

470
00:24:36,387 --> 00:24:37,476
We are lawyers.

471
00:24:40,348 --> 00:24:41,741
If there's nothing else..

472
00:24:41,828 --> 00:24:43,873
[clears throat]

473
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:46,136
- Jane.
- What?

474
00:24:46,223 --> 00:24:47,703
I'm trying to figure out
what this number

475
00:24:47,790 --> 00:24:49,444
on Castro's desk means.

476
00:24:49,531 --> 00:24:51,402
The only thing I can find are
a couple of references

477
00:24:51,490 --> 00:24:53,361
to some sort
of high-end social club.

478
00:24:53,448 --> 00:24:56,190
Any idea why they'd name it
19:24?

479
00:24:56,277 --> 00:24:57,278
The Bible.

480
00:24:58,540 --> 00:25:01,064
Matthew 19:24.

481
00:25:01,151 --> 00:25:02,762
"Again I say unto you,
it is easier for a camel

482
00:25:02,849 --> 00:25:04,459
to go through the eye
of a needle than it is"--

483
00:25:04,546 --> 00:25:06,940
"Than for a rich man to enter
the kingdom of heaven."

484
00:25:07,027 --> 00:25:09,333
- Yes.
- That's weird.

485
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:10,857
Well, it's an ironic joke.

486
00:25:10,944 --> 00:25:13,816
Since the rich
won't get into heaven

487
00:25:13,903 --> 00:25:15,426
they should enjoy
their life on earth.

488
00:25:15,514 --> 00:25:17,124
Okay, but why did Castro care?

489
00:25:17,211 --> 00:25:18,865
He wasn't a member,
far as I can tell.

490
00:25:20,606 --> 00:25:22,564
Mm. Of course he wasn't.

491
00:25:27,395 --> 00:25:29,353
Well, that clears things up.

492
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:31,007
[elevator bell dings]

493
00:25:31,094 --> 00:25:32,879
Oh, guys!

494
00:25:32,966 --> 00:25:37,623
[clears throat]
Tell me about, uh, 19:24.

495
00:25:37,710 --> 00:25:39,015
And don't deny
you're members

496
00:25:39,102 --> 00:25:41,583
'cause I can see it
all over your faces.

497
00:25:41,670 --> 00:25:43,803
Well, you know,
it's-it's a club.

498
00:25:43,890 --> 00:25:45,108
Small, private.

499
00:25:45,195 --> 00:25:46,632
For the filthy rich?

500
00:25:46,719 --> 00:25:49,156
- For people of means.
- Oh.

501
00:25:49,243 --> 00:25:50,940
No one is supposed
to talk about it.

502
00:25:51,027 --> 00:25:52,812
First rule of fight club
and all that.

503
00:25:52,899 --> 00:25:53,900
Okay.

504
00:25:54,901 --> 00:25:56,380
Who else is members?

505
00:25:56,467 --> 00:25:59,253
- Breitler? Your niece?
- Both of them.

506
00:25:59,340 --> 00:26:00,994
Lots of people from my firm,
other firms.

507
00:26:01,081 --> 00:26:02,778
So i-if your niece
was a member

508
00:26:02,865 --> 00:26:06,565
then surely, uh, Antonio Castro
was asked about joining.

509
00:26:06,652 --> 00:26:07,870
- 'Yes.'
- Yeah.

510
00:26:07,957 --> 00:26:10,307
Shortly before
he disappeared.

511
00:26:10,394 --> 00:26:13,920
Right. And to become a member
of this, um, fancy

512
00:26:14,007 --> 00:26:15,704
elite little club, uh

513
00:26:15,791 --> 00:26:17,837
I'm assuming you need to
have some kind of a hazing.

514
00:26:17,924 --> 00:26:20,230
[chuckles]
Yes.

515
00:26:20,317 --> 00:26:22,798
Prospective members are tapped
by current members

516
00:26:22,885 --> 00:26:26,193
and forced to do all sorts
of embarrassing things.

517
00:26:26,280 --> 00:26:27,586
[laughs]
Oh.

518
00:26:27,673 --> 00:26:29,805
- Good fun.
- Sure.

519
00:26:29,892 --> 00:26:32,460
This charity event
that Antonio Castro attended

520
00:26:32,547 --> 00:26:35,724
the night he disappeared,
was that held at the club?

521
00:26:35,811 --> 00:26:38,379
As a matter of fact, it was.
Antonio was there as a guest.

522
00:26:38,466 --> 00:26:39,946
[elevator bell dings]
Mm.

523
00:26:40,033 --> 00:26:41,817
You interested in membership,
Mr. Jane?

524
00:26:41,904 --> 00:26:43,036
- Me?
- Yeah.

525
00:26:43,123 --> 00:26:45,473
Uh, no. No.
Not the club type.

526
00:26:45,560 --> 00:26:46,866
- Oh.
- Thank you.

527
00:26:49,172 --> 00:26:51,261
I've got an accountant
looking at Castro's trades

528
00:26:51,348 --> 00:26:52,741
but they're so complicated, he
can barely make sense of them.

529
00:26:52,828 --> 00:26:54,221
What else do we have?

530
00:26:54,308 --> 00:26:55,918
I talked to 13 people
who met with Castro

531
00:26:56,005 --> 00:26:58,268
before he disappeared.
Nothing unusual or suspicious.

532
00:26:58,355 --> 00:27:00,357
I've been through
all the forensic evidence

533
00:27:00,444 --> 00:27:02,969
SFPD gathered from Castro's
apartment and office.

534
00:27:03,056 --> 00:27:05,058
- Nothing jumps out.
- Time line?

535
00:27:05,145 --> 00:27:07,669
- 'Nothing that leads anywhere.'
- 'We don't have anything.'

536
00:27:07,756 --> 00:27:10,019
We have something. We just
don't know what it is yet.

537
00:27:10,106 --> 00:27:11,630
I do. Let's go.

538
00:27:11,717 --> 00:27:14,110
- Where?
- Uh, 19:24 club.

539
00:27:14,197 --> 00:27:16,896
I convinced Marcy Victor
to throw an emergency meeting.

540
00:27:16,983 --> 00:27:19,072
- What for?
- To solve the case, of course.

541
00:27:20,769 --> 00:27:22,205
Where'd you get this?

542
00:27:23,163 --> 00:27:24,643
[indistinct chattering]

543
00:27:24,730 --> 00:27:26,122
[Jane]
'Do you have
the exits covered?'

544
00:27:26,209 --> 00:27:28,124
Rigsby, Cho, and Van Pelt
are by the doors.

545
00:27:28,211 --> 00:27:29,822
Okay.

546
00:27:29,909 --> 00:27:31,388
Hello, everybody.

547
00:27:31,475 --> 00:27:33,390
Thank you for coming.

548
00:27:33,477 --> 00:27:35,392
I'm Patrick Jane with the CBI.

549
00:27:35,479 --> 00:27:37,786
As you've been told,
we're investigating the death

550
00:27:37,873 --> 00:27:41,398
of Marcy Victor's fiance
Antonio Castro.

551
00:27:41,485 --> 00:27:45,533
What you haven't been told is
that the killer is in this room.

552
00:27:45,620 --> 00:27:48,188
All I need to identify him

553
00:27:48,275 --> 00:27:50,190
are the answers
to a few questions.

554
00:27:50,277 --> 00:27:53,193
- What kind of questions?
- Very simple ones.

555
00:27:53,280 --> 00:27:56,500
Uh, you, ma'am,
the frayed thread on your sleeve

556
00:27:56,587 --> 00:28:00,243
'plus last season's shoes
suggest money trouble. True?'

557
00:28:00,330 --> 00:28:02,550
No. No, I've..
No, we're fine.

558
00:28:02,637 --> 00:28:03,899
[chuckles]
Uh, we're fine.

559
00:28:03,986 --> 00:28:05,422
See? A lie.

560
00:28:06,597 --> 00:28:08,599
Did you kill Antonio Castro?

561
00:28:08,687 --> 00:28:10,253
I didn't even know him.

562
00:28:10,340 --> 00:28:11,559
And there's the truth.

563
00:28:12,691 --> 00:28:14,475
When I watch you tell a lie

564
00:28:14,562 --> 00:28:17,739
it helps me determine whether
you're telling the truth.

565
00:28:17,826 --> 00:28:21,612
For example, Marcy here
is sleeping with Ian.

566
00:28:21,700 --> 00:28:24,833
You're not committed to him
emotionally, and you don't think

567
00:28:24,920 --> 00:28:27,357
he's half the man that
your fiance was, do you?

568
00:28:27,444 --> 00:28:28,924
No, that's not true.

569
00:28:29,011 --> 00:28:31,057
A lie.
Thank you very much.

570
00:28:31,144 --> 00:28:33,407
- Did you kill Antonio?
- Of course not.

571
00:28:33,494 --> 00:28:35,017
And there's the truth.

572
00:28:35,104 --> 00:28:36,932
- Is this some kind of joke?
- Please be patient.

573
00:28:37,019 --> 00:28:38,542
Take a seat, ma'am.

574
00:28:38,629 --> 00:28:40,370
Mr. Marx, you're more
than a little twisted

575
00:28:40,457 --> 00:28:42,808
'in the bedroom,
aren't you?'

576
00:28:42,895 --> 00:28:45,288
'Latex and whips are your thing,
are they not?'

577
00:28:45,375 --> 00:28:46,725
[indistinct murmur]

578
00:28:46,812 --> 00:28:48,335
Not at all.

579
00:28:48,422 --> 00:28:50,119
Did you kill Antonio Castro?

580
00:28:50,206 --> 00:28:52,513
No, I did not.

581
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:54,689
Hmm. Uh, you, sir.

582
00:28:54,776 --> 00:28:56,996
[chuckles]
If you think
I'm saying a word

583
00:28:57,083 --> 00:28:59,433
without a lawyer on either side
of me, you're crazy.

584
00:28:59,520 --> 00:29:02,218
And any of you that put up with
this is as crazy as he is.

585
00:29:03,524 --> 00:29:06,048
Ladies and gentlemen.

586
00:29:06,135 --> 00:29:08,921
If everybody could just stay
in the room, please.

587
00:29:09,008 --> 00:29:10,749
[indistinct chattering]

588
00:29:10,836 --> 00:29:12,663
I don't understand.
What were you trying to do?

589
00:29:14,709 --> 00:29:16,885
The plan was to, uh

590
00:29:16,972 --> 00:29:19,453
scare the killer
into making a run for it.

591
00:29:19,540 --> 00:29:20,846
You thought that would work?

592
00:29:20,933 --> 00:29:25,198
Certainly had a fair chance,
I thought.

593
00:29:25,285 --> 00:29:27,330
I'm gonna go tell Rigsby
and the others to stand down.

594
00:29:27,417 --> 00:29:28,767
You stay here.

595
00:29:35,730 --> 00:29:38,211
Yeah, it was crazy. I'll tell
you about it when I get there.

596
00:29:38,298 --> 00:29:39,516
Alright. Bye.

597
00:29:39,603 --> 00:29:42,171
- Marx!
- Mr. Jane.

598
00:29:42,258 --> 00:29:43,956
That didn't work out for you
so well, did it?

599
00:29:44,043 --> 00:29:45,958
No, it went pretty much
as planned.

600
00:29:46,045 --> 00:29:48,003
- The case is cracked.
- Oh, yeah?

601
00:29:48,090 --> 00:29:49,788
- Is that your car?
- Yep.

602
00:29:49,875 --> 00:29:51,659
- Wow. That's a nice ride.
- Thanks.

603
00:29:51,746 --> 00:29:54,836
Let's take a little drive.
I'll tell you all about it.

604
00:29:54,923 --> 00:29:57,143
- Ride where?
- Oh, not far.

605
00:29:57,230 --> 00:29:59,232
I mean, don't you want to be
the first to know who did it?

606
00:30:01,277 --> 00:30:02,844
Okay.

607
00:30:02,931 --> 00:30:05,891
- What's in the bag?
- Oh, a baby monitor.

608
00:30:05,978 --> 00:30:07,893
[chuckles]

609
00:30:07,980 --> 00:30:09,416
I-I'll explain later.

610
00:30:23,212 --> 00:30:24,823
Turn right up here.

611
00:30:26,912 --> 00:30:29,175
So how exactly did that mess
back at the club

612
00:30:29,262 --> 00:30:30,393
work out for you,
Mr. Jane?

613
00:30:32,961 --> 00:30:34,180
Wasn't looking for a killer.

614
00:30:35,268 --> 00:30:37,487
I was looking for a liar.

615
00:30:37,574 --> 00:30:40,055
Someone that can answer
an embarrassing question

616
00:30:40,142 --> 00:30:42,101
in public
without getting flustered.

617
00:30:42,188 --> 00:30:43,537
Hmm.

618
00:30:43,624 --> 00:30:44,930
Why was that important?

619
00:30:45,017 --> 00:30:48,281
Castro's killer was
a vicious sadist.

620
00:30:48,368 --> 00:30:51,458
Someone that truly enjoyed
watching other people's pain.

621
00:30:51,545 --> 00:30:52,894
He can't show it.

622
00:30:52,981 --> 00:30:55,288
Ergo, a first-rate liar.

623
00:30:56,376 --> 00:30:57,377
And you found him?

624
00:30:58,030 --> 00:30:59,379
Sure.

625
00:31:01,511 --> 00:31:02,904
It was you.

626
00:31:02,991 --> 00:31:04,036
Turn, uh, here.

627
00:31:05,298 --> 00:31:06,995
Me?

628
00:31:07,082 --> 00:31:10,694
I noticed when we first talked,
you and, uh, Victor and Breitler

629
00:31:10,781 --> 00:31:12,261
said that Castro hadn't done
anything wrong

630
00:31:12,348 --> 00:31:13,959
and I could tell
the other two were lying

631
00:31:14,046 --> 00:31:15,743
but you seemed
to be telling the truth.

632
00:31:17,179 --> 00:31:19,312
And not too many people
fool me like that.

633
00:31:20,791 --> 00:31:22,837
So I tested you again
at the club.

634
00:31:24,099 --> 00:31:26,058
- Oh, yeah?
- Mm.

635
00:31:26,145 --> 00:31:29,235
- How'd I do?
- Outstanding.

636
00:31:29,322 --> 00:31:30,845
One of the best liars
I've ever seen.

637
00:31:32,934 --> 00:31:34,544
It explains your success
in sales

638
00:31:34,631 --> 00:31:36,590
and, uh,
the kinky sex life.

639
00:31:36,677 --> 00:31:40,028
Just, uh, just-just pull in
a little up here.

640
00:31:51,648 --> 00:31:53,041
None of that means
I killed Antonio.

641
00:31:54,347 --> 00:31:55,696
Sure, it does.

642
00:31:55,783 --> 00:31:57,785
- I can prove it.
- How?

643
00:31:59,134 --> 00:32:00,570
Walk with me,
and I'll show you.

644
00:32:03,312 --> 00:32:05,488
[intense music]

645
00:32:07,795 --> 00:32:09,057
Castro didn't take the money.

646
00:32:10,189 --> 00:32:11,494
Too much of a boy scout.

647
00:32:12,931 --> 00:32:15,107
It was you that stole it.

648
00:32:15,194 --> 00:32:17,370
Castro had figured out
that someone was stealing

649
00:32:17,457 --> 00:32:19,894
so he, uh,
he came to you.

650
00:32:19,981 --> 00:32:22,462
Why?
Because you're his boss.

651
00:32:22,549 --> 00:32:25,465
What he didn't realize,
that you were also the thief.

652
00:32:25,552 --> 00:32:27,293
Something's wrong
with the future tallies.

653
00:32:27,380 --> 00:32:29,077
I-I just can't
put my finger on what.

654
00:32:29,164 --> 00:32:30,296
Alright.

655
00:32:32,037 --> 00:32:33,386
Okay. I'll look into it.

656
00:32:33,473 --> 00:32:35,562
[Jane]
Then you had to kill him.

657
00:32:35,649 --> 00:32:37,825
How exactly did I do that?

658
00:32:37,912 --> 00:32:40,349
Kind of brilliantly,
I have to say.

659
00:32:40,436 --> 00:32:42,612
[Jane]
The 19:24 club
was the ticket.

660
00:32:42,699 --> 00:32:45,528
Castro was gonna join, and the night the charity function

661
00:32:45,615 --> 00:32:48,096
took place, you told him
he was gonna be initiated

662
00:32:48,183 --> 00:32:49,706
but he had to be hazed first.

663
00:32:49,793 --> 00:32:51,839
- You ready?
- Now?

664
00:32:51,926 --> 00:32:54,233
Shh. It's supposed to be
a surprise.

665
00:32:54,320 --> 00:32:56,670
Get your car
and meet me around the corner.

666
00:32:56,757 --> 00:32:59,238
I'm gonna drive you there.
But don't tell anybody

667
00:32:59,325 --> 00:33:01,022
where you're going
or what you're doing, okay?

668
00:33:01,109 --> 00:33:03,677
Exactly how embarrassing
is this gonna be?

669
00:33:03,764 --> 00:33:06,723
Awesomely, my brother.

670
00:33:06,810 --> 00:33:10,423
But I do have a
little help here for you.

671
00:33:12,947 --> 00:33:14,557
Make it go a lot easier.

672
00:33:15,994 --> 00:33:17,299
Trust me.

673
00:33:19,432 --> 00:33:20,737
So you took him
to the factory.

674
00:33:21,956 --> 00:33:23,436
You walked him downstairs..

675
00:33:25,742 --> 00:33:26,830
...to the basement.

676
00:33:30,747 --> 00:33:32,227
He did everything you asked.

677
00:33:35,752 --> 00:33:39,713
What the hell?
Yeah. Be careful..

678
00:33:39,800 --> 00:33:41,845
- Yeah.
- It'll be alright.

679
00:33:41,932 --> 00:33:43,456
Drawers too.

680
00:33:43,543 --> 00:33:44,979
- In the box.
- Get in the box.

681
00:33:45,066 --> 00:33:46,589
You can do it.

682
00:33:54,858 --> 00:33:56,686
Alright, Antonio,
you good?

683
00:33:56,773 --> 00:33:58,384
- Yeah. What now?
- Just you wait.

684
00:34:00,473 --> 00:34:03,128
[door hinges creaks]

685
00:34:06,783 --> 00:34:08,002
And then you went
to his house

686
00:34:08,089 --> 00:34:09,917
and sent e-mails
from his computer

687
00:34:10,004 --> 00:34:12,311
so that no one could connect
his disappearance

688
00:34:12,398 --> 00:34:13,790
with the club or you.

689
00:34:13,877 --> 00:34:16,402
Mm. Well, this-that sounds
pretty cool.

690
00:34:17,664 --> 00:34:20,058
Except Castro was a thief.

691
00:34:20,145 --> 00:34:22,712
All the trades were
on his account.

692
00:34:22,799 --> 00:34:25,541
You had weeks
to fake that data.

693
00:34:25,628 --> 00:34:27,326
I bet that wasn't
very hard for you.

694
00:34:27,413 --> 00:34:30,938
Ooh. Gosh, I must be
a very bad man.

695
00:34:32,896 --> 00:34:34,072
Vicious and heartless.

696
00:34:36,030 --> 00:34:38,641
You gave Castro the most
terrifying death possible.

697
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:44,734
You're evil.

698
00:34:44,821 --> 00:34:46,127
Where's your proof?

699
00:34:49,348 --> 00:34:51,306
- You're gonna confess to me.
- Oh, yeah?

700
00:34:51,393 --> 00:34:53,221
- Yeah.
- Why would I do that?

701
00:35:00,402 --> 00:35:02,274
What, you're gonna scare me
into saying that I did it?

702
00:35:02,361 --> 00:35:03,318
That's your plan?

703
00:35:07,148 --> 00:35:08,497
Yeah, that's the plan.

704
00:35:09,672 --> 00:35:11,326
[dramatic music]

705
00:35:14,851 --> 00:35:16,288
[birds chirping]

706
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:30,737
Hey.

707
00:35:30,824 --> 00:35:32,391
Good morning,Lisbon.

708
00:35:32,478 --> 00:35:33,914
'Got your message.'

709
00:35:35,916 --> 00:35:37,831
What are you doing here?

710
00:35:37,918 --> 00:35:39,137
Resting.

711
00:35:40,399 --> 00:35:41,965
Solved the case.

712
00:35:42,052 --> 00:35:43,053
Really?

713
00:35:43,141 --> 00:35:44,968
Yeah, it was Benjamin Marx.

714
00:35:45,055 --> 00:35:46,840
He stole the money,
killed Castro

715
00:35:46,927 --> 00:35:48,407
and pinned
the theft on him.

716
00:35:48,494 --> 00:35:51,061
- How do you know that?
- He's gonna confess.

717
00:35:51,149 --> 00:35:53,325
- Gonna confess?
- Mm. Very soon, I think.

718
00:35:55,370 --> 00:35:56,893
Where is he?

719
00:35:57,546 --> 00:35:58,852
Uh..

720
00:36:01,246 --> 00:36:02,812
...he's here.

721
00:36:05,598 --> 00:36:07,382
[Jane]
'Oh. Sorry.'

722
00:36:07,469 --> 00:36:09,471
[grunts]
Somebody help me!

723
00:36:09,558 --> 00:36:12,213
[grunts]
Jane, help me! Please!

724
00:36:12,300 --> 00:36:13,997
Yeah, he-he does go on.

725
00:36:15,303 --> 00:36:17,479
Y-you locked him
in a coffin?

726
00:36:17,566 --> 00:36:20,917
Yeah. Give him a taste
of his own medicine.

727
00:36:21,004 --> 00:36:23,268
Where is he?
How long has he been in there?

728
00:36:23,355 --> 00:36:24,921
We have to get him out.
He'll die.

729
00:36:25,008 --> 00:36:27,489
- Not until he confesses.
- Jane, you can't do this.

730
00:36:27,576 --> 00:36:29,012
Sure, I can.
Watch this.

731
00:36:32,015 --> 00:36:34,540
- Marx, you hear me?
- Jane! Jane, help, me, please.

732
00:36:34,627 --> 00:36:35,802
Please! I can't breathe!

733
00:36:35,889 --> 00:36:38,065
No problem. Just admit
to killing Castro.

734
00:36:38,935 --> 00:36:40,241
[grunts]

735
00:36:42,025 --> 00:36:43,940
- Marx.
- No!

736
00:36:45,159 --> 00:36:46,552
Okay. Die.

737
00:36:48,858 --> 00:36:50,643
Jane, tell me where he is.

738
00:36:51,644 --> 00:36:52,949
I mean it.

739
00:36:55,822 --> 00:36:57,563
- Marx, you change your mind?
- Jane.

740
00:36:57,650 --> 00:37:01,219
Oh, God! Oh! Oh! Oh!

741
00:37:01,306 --> 00:37:02,872
Jane!

742
00:37:02,959 --> 00:37:04,613
Okay, I'm gonna walk away
from here

743
00:37:04,700 --> 00:37:05,962
and no one
will ever find you.

744
00:37:07,225 --> 00:37:09,009
Yes! Yes! Yes!

745
00:37:09,096 --> 00:37:11,794
I did it!
Yeah, I-I killed Antonio.

746
00:37:11,881 --> 00:37:13,579
'You locked him in that box.'

747
00:37:13,666 --> 00:37:15,755
- 'And you left him to die.'
- I did. I did. I did.

748
00:37:15,842 --> 00:37:17,539
Please. Get me out of here.

749
00:37:17,626 --> 00:37:19,106
You must have kept a trophy.
Where is it?

750
00:37:19,193 --> 00:37:21,282
Yes! Okay. Okay. Okay.

751
00:37:23,371 --> 00:37:26,287
His key ring. His key ring.
There's an army insignia on it.

752
00:37:26,374 --> 00:37:30,073
In a box, okay? Top shelf,
bedroom closet. Okay? Please.

753
00:37:30,160 --> 00:37:33,947
Well, there you go.
He's, uh, over by that tree.

754
00:37:35,731 --> 00:37:37,994
I need an ambulance
at the Willow Wood Cemetery

755
00:37:38,081 --> 00:37:39,518
as soon as possible.

756
00:37:39,605 --> 00:37:41,084
God, Jane!

757
00:37:44,218 --> 00:37:45,959
[indistinct police radio
chatter]

758
00:37:51,965 --> 00:37:53,271
What'd you find?

759
00:37:54,968 --> 00:37:56,317
Okay.

760
00:37:56,404 --> 00:37:58,101
[Lisbon]
'Cho said
they found the key ring'

761
00:37:58,188 --> 00:38:00,278
'in the exact place
Marx said it would be.'

762
00:38:02,236 --> 00:38:03,629
'Let's go see Wainwright.'

763
00:38:04,847 --> 00:38:06,196
'Come on.'

764
00:38:14,466 --> 00:38:16,163
You know, I really
can't see the problem.

765
00:38:16,250 --> 00:38:17,686
I closed the case.

766
00:38:17,773 --> 00:38:19,993
You solved what-what case?
There is no case.

767
00:38:20,080 --> 00:38:21,951
No judge is ever gonna let
that confession stand.

768
00:38:22,038 --> 00:38:23,910
They're gonna throw out
the key chain also.

769
00:38:23,997 --> 00:38:25,259
Marx is
never gonna see a trial.

770
00:38:25,346 --> 00:38:27,479
Meh. Let the lawyers
sort it out.

771
00:38:27,566 --> 00:38:29,524
Cho and the accountants
have already found evidence

772
00:38:29,611 --> 00:38:31,047
in Castro's
trading accounts

773
00:38:31,134 --> 00:38:32,875
that it was Marx
who stole the money.

774
00:38:32,962 --> 00:38:35,835
'The DA is gonna charge him
with theft and security fraud.'

775
00:38:35,922 --> 00:38:37,619
Now I think we can build
a murder case

776
00:38:37,706 --> 00:38:39,708
on top of that
without the confession.

777
00:38:39,795 --> 00:38:42,276
- See?
- I don't care.

778
00:38:42,363 --> 00:38:44,104
You assaulted Marx.

779
00:38:44,191 --> 00:38:45,540
Sac PD wants to arrest you.

780
00:38:45,627 --> 00:38:49,239
Marx had a gun.
That's self-defense.

781
00:38:49,327 --> 00:38:51,285
I think a jury
will believe me.

782
00:38:51,372 --> 00:38:53,548
Jane is under a tremendous
amount of stress.

783
00:38:53,635 --> 00:38:55,158
I'm fine, thank you.

784
00:38:55,245 --> 00:38:57,683
Look, you tortured the guy,
for Christ sakes.

785
00:38:57,770 --> 00:39:01,077
Jane, you tortured him.

786
00:39:01,164 --> 00:39:05,212
Do you have any notion
how that makes the CBI look?

787
00:39:05,299 --> 00:39:08,563
I gave an evil psychopath
justice.

788
00:39:08,650 --> 00:39:11,697
Now does anyone here think that
Marx didn't deserve what he got?

789
00:39:11,784 --> 00:39:13,655
I think he deserved it.

790
00:39:13,742 --> 00:39:15,135
Thank you, Grace.
Anyone else?

791
00:39:15,222 --> 00:39:17,790
Cho? Rigsby?

792
00:39:17,877 --> 00:39:20,836
- No, I get it. It's okay.
- Jane, you crossed the line.

793
00:39:20,923 --> 00:39:22,664
Yes, of course I did.
I crossed the line.

794
00:39:22,751 --> 00:39:26,451
I had to to get Marx. It was
the only way to get Marx.

795
00:39:26,538 --> 00:39:27,887
If you don't get the bad guy,
then what's the point

796
00:39:27,974 --> 00:39:29,410
of all this?

797
00:39:30,846 --> 00:39:32,239
I'm not gonna apologize.

798
00:39:32,326 --> 00:39:34,502
Maybe you should take
a time-out.

799
00:39:34,589 --> 00:39:36,199
You know what?
That's a good idea.

800
00:39:37,723 --> 00:39:39,507
You're suspended
as of this moment.

801
00:39:39,594 --> 00:39:41,204
You're not gonna come
into this office.

802
00:39:41,291 --> 00:39:43,903
You're not gonna have
anything to do with the CBI.

803
00:39:43,990 --> 00:39:45,992
There'll be a hearing
on your status in 30 days.

804
00:39:49,430 --> 00:39:52,172
And I want you to know
I'm gonna personally recommend

805
00:39:52,259 --> 00:39:55,349
CBI discontinue you permanently
as a consultant.

806
00:39:55,436 --> 00:39:57,699
I understand that you're under
some distress, Jane

807
00:39:59,135 --> 00:40:01,399
but youshamed us today.

808
00:40:03,966 --> 00:40:05,881
And I'm not gonna
let that happen again.

809
00:40:09,015 --> 00:40:10,495
You're a pathetic little boy.

810
00:40:12,540 --> 00:40:14,020
- Excuse me?
- You're a child.

811
00:40:16,196 --> 00:40:18,677
Daddy was mean to you.
He thought you were soft.

812
00:40:18,764 --> 00:40:19,721
You're a mama's boy.

813
00:40:19,808 --> 00:40:20,983
I'm s..
This has--

814
00:40:21,070 --> 00:40:23,029
Oh, go home, little boy.
Go home to mama.

815
00:40:23,116 --> 00:40:24,334
'Go on. Off to mama.'

816
00:40:24,422 --> 00:40:26,380
- Jane, stop.
- Oh, come on.

817
00:40:26,467 --> 00:40:29,078
'He's a pathetic, little,
mewling mama's boy.'

818
00:40:29,165 --> 00:40:32,168
He's still got her milk
on his lip. Look at him. Huh?

819
00:40:32,255 --> 00:40:33,605
[chuckles]

820
00:40:35,345 --> 00:40:36,738
- Hey! Hey!
- Whoa, boss!

821
00:40:36,825 --> 00:40:37,826
[Cho]
Take it easy.
Take it easy.

822
00:40:37,913 --> 00:40:39,219
Come on. I'm gonna kill you.

823
00:40:39,306 --> 00:40:40,481
- 'Boss.'
- Get off me.

824
00:40:40,568 --> 00:40:42,657
[indistinct chattering]

825
00:40:44,485 --> 00:40:46,269
- Come on!
- Okay. Okay. Get off of me.

826
00:40:46,356 --> 00:40:47,706
Get off of me.

827
00:40:48,707 --> 00:40:50,317
[intense music]

828
00:41:08,074 --> 00:41:11,207
On second thought,
you're not suspended.

829
00:41:11,294 --> 00:41:12,600
You're fired.

830
00:41:13,688 --> 00:41:15,298
Get out of here right now.

831
00:41:17,083 --> 00:41:18,693
[instrumental music]

832
00:41:23,176 --> 00:41:24,482
Jane.

833
00:41:29,835 --> 00:41:31,750
Jane, don't do anything rash.

834
00:41:31,837 --> 00:41:33,447
We can work this out.
We'll fix it.

835
00:41:35,014 --> 00:41:36,755
I doubt that.

836
00:41:36,842 --> 00:41:39,061
I'll come by your place tonight.
We'll talk it out, okay?

837
00:41:41,803 --> 00:41:43,196
You're sweet.

838
00:41:44,197 --> 00:41:45,590
Let me help you.

839
00:41:46,678 --> 00:41:48,331
[elevator bell dings]

840
00:41:51,160 --> 00:41:52,858
[elevator whirring]

841
00:42:02,432 --> 00:42:03,521
[theme music]

